27
00:02:18,542 --> 00:02:20,164
So that's one big thing that happened.

28
00:02:20,814 --> 00:02:25,572
Another thing coming from OpenAI is this launch of MCP events.

29
00:02:26,222 --> 00:02:34,532
So I presume a lot of you here know what is MCP, but for those that don't, it stands for Model Context Protocol.

30
00:02:34,766 --> 00:02:41,700
This is a way for, in this case, ChatGPT to connect with other apps and then use their information or tools.

31
00:02:42,158 --> 00:02:49,636
So previously, ChatGPT could check a connected app when you explicitly asked it to.

32
00:02:49,902 --> 00:02:56,644
And it also supported some event-triggered tasks for things with Gmail, with Slack, with GitHub.

33
00:02:57,280 --> 00:03:11,840
But with this new announcement of MCP events, it's a way for developers to let their own MCP connected apps send updates directly to ChatGPT when a subscribed event happens.

34
00:03:12,142 --> 00:03:20,320
So you can tell ChatGPT what to monitor and then how to respond through all of your connected apps, and then it will act on its own.

35
00:03:20,654 --> 00:03:23,044
So that's the idea of MCP events.

36
00:03:23,950 --> 00:03:30,820
Another thing that happened is that OpenAI says it's going to now begin adding text grain.

37
00:03:31,246 --> 00:03:41,572
This is the name that they gave to an invisible watermark that they're going to put inside of Chat GPT or codex text inside of the European Union.

38
00:03:41,902 --> 00:03:49,348
So we know that LLMs work with statistical patterns and probability to predict the next token, the next word.

39
00:03:49,614 --> 00:03:57,604
And they'll now be embedding a signal into the word choices to help identify OpenAI generated text.

40
00:03:58,158 --> 00:04:03,684
This is very similar, if you're familiar with it, with what Anthropic did back in August.

41
00:04:04,206 --> 00:04:06,960
So Anthropic did something very similar with Claude.

42
00:04:07,342 --> 00:04:15,716
They have their own watermark in Claude generated text within the European Union, within the EU, because of the changing regulations here.

43
00:04:16,398 --> 00:04:21,552
Now, as with all of these text watermarks, they are not perfect.

44
00:04:21,712 --> 00:04:23,392
This is not a perfect solution.

45
00:04:23,472 --> 00:04:25,508
I don't know that there necessarily is one.

46
00:04:26,032 --> 00:04:30,512
The detectors that you use, the signal detectors, can have false positives.

47
00:04:30,862 --> 00:04:33,792
So that means reporting a watermark when there is none.

48
00:04:33,998 --> 00:04:35,844
And they can also have false negatives.

49
00:04:36,032 --> 00:04:39,300
So missing a watermark that is present in the text.

50
00:04:39,886 --> 00:04:46,212
And also there are two major risks that you have to have in mind with these types of text watermarks.

51
00:04:46,606 --> 00:04:51,524
And that's one that shorter text, it's much harder to detect it within that.

52
00:04:51,854 --> 00:04:54,596
And then editing can weaken the watermark.

53
00:04:54,958 --> 00:05:01,860
So the watermark is not a guarantee, but it's a clue that OpenAI may have been involved in that text.

54
00:05:02,478 --> 00:05:04,352
So that's all I've got for OpenAI.

55
00:05:04,432 --> 00:05:07,232
Let's move on to their biggest competitor, Anthropic.

56
00:05:07,392 --> 00:05:09,220
What has been happening with Anthropic?

57
00:05:09,902 --> 00:05:14,020
Well, first, they are adding two visual tools to Claude.

58
00:05:14,318 --> 00:05:17,632
You've got the announcement of Claude Dashboards and Claude Motion.

59
00:05:18,126 --> 00:05:23,332
So dashboards can link to platforms like BigQuery, Databricks, et cetera.

60
00:05:23,552 --> 00:05:34,692
And then you can use plain language to ask questions about your company data and then generate these interactive dashboards that also update whenever your data does.

61
00:05:35,118 --> 00:05:40,036
Just like you can see on the screen now, this is an example of a dashboard built inside of Claude.

62
00:05:40,750 --> 00:05:48,112
Now, I have some opinions on this because I've worked a lot within data visualization and data storytelling in the past in my career.

63
00:05:48,352 --> 00:06:00,996
And I think if if Claude dashboards starts to be used in companies, I think there is initially a real risk of creating chaos for the BI teams, the business intelligence teams.

64
00:06:01,454 --> 00:06:08,868
If anyone can ask questions about data and then build their own dashboards, that that I think could be both a good and a bad thing.

65
00:06:09,102 --> 00:06:18,788
Just because you ask for something doesn't mean your query matches the business definition that it should, or you might not be including the right context.

66
00:06:19,374 --> 00:06:26,756
Things like these are things that the data and the BI team would always have in mind and they would always be careful whenever they're building dashboards.

67
00:06:27,022 --> 00:06:36,452
But the moment you give that power to other people not as familiar with the data, they can make a lot of mistakes or not be interpreting or using the data the right way.

68
00:06:36,814 --> 00:06:45,872
And I think as it is it becomes easier to interact with data through natural language, then BI teams, in the end, their work is going to change.

69
00:06:46,032 --> 00:06:50,992
I don't think they're going to be eliminated by any means, but I think things are going to change.

70
00:06:51,278 --> 00:06:59,716
So people might handle in any person in the company, they might handle more simple exploratory questions themselves about the data.

71
00:07:00,142 --> 00:07:15,332
while the BI teams can focus on this bigger picture work, and they can make sure that they're supporting with trusted data and that they're creating these good shared metric definitions and still building the main dashboards that people should rely on for the big decision making.

72
00:07:15,822 --> 00:07:29,232
But I do think there's a real risk of very quick unofficial dashboards getting shared across the company and being treated as the source of truth and very possibly spreading incorrect information.

73
00:07:29,486 --> 00:07:30,512
So we have to be very careful.

74
00:07:30,592 --> 00:07:39,748
I think the announcement of clawed dashboards is a double-edged sword, depending on how you manage manage it and who you give permission to use these types of things.

75
00:07:40,270 --> 00:07:41,632
So that's on the dashboard side.

76
00:07:41,774 --> 00:07:45,828
And the other thing they announced along with that is what is called Claude Motion.

77
00:07:46,192 --> 00:07:51,364
So this turns reports and charts into editable animations.

78
00:07:51,758 --> 00:08:07,236
Like imagine, for example, if you have a complex report or a complex chart, then motion can turn it into a short explainer with animated text and graphics that you can also modify yourself and then export as a video file and use that.

79
00:08:07,854 --> 00:08:11,072
Both Claude Dashboards and Claude Motion are new.

80
00:08:11,152 --> 00:08:15,172
So they are in beta mode and they're offered only within certain plans.

81
00:08:15,758 --> 00:08:26,372
But along with this announcement, there was another smaller announcement, which is that Claude Docs, Slides, and Design have now left beta and they are available on all the plans.

82
00:08:26,670 --> 00:08:30,340
So eventually as well, we'll see the same thing happen with dashboards and motion.

83
00:08:31,150 --> 00:08:36,704
So, on an entirely different note, another thing happened with Anthropic this past week.

84
00:08:36,864 --> 00:08:41,504
They are continuing to have problems controlling their AI systems.

85
00:08:41,664 --> 00:08:43,984
I know we've talked about this on a lot of the shows.

86
00:08:44,224 --> 00:08:47,584
I have, Vicky has mentioned it as well, my co-host.

87
00:08:48,014 --> 00:08:52,036
So the systems are a little bit difficult to control at times.

88
00:08:52,544 --> 00:08:59,876
And Anthropic found more cases where Claude worked around restrictions during tests.

89
00:09:00,622 --> 00:09:02,304
And this was including some things.

90
00:09:02,414 --> 00:09:10,980
They gave a list of different examples, but they found that there were there were instances when it was copying server files from a university.

91
00:09:11,406 --> 00:09:16,708
There was another case where it was submitting these fake fabricated forms on government websites.

92
00:09:17,102 --> 00:09:22,436
So all of these are serious, serious missteps, but it's nothing too damaging too far.

93
00:09:22,798 --> 00:09:37,504
And so because of all this happening and this lack of control on the AI systems and the agents, Anthropic has now turned off live internet access in their internal tests until they're better able to monitor their systems.

94
00:09:37,774 --> 00:09:47,044
So their systems will essentially be turned off from the internet to try to prevent them from accessing the internet and doing things that they were not instructed or not supposed to do.

95
00:09:47,854 --> 00:10:04,384
Now, the last thing I wanted to highlight with Anthropic is that they published this really interesting research examining which US jobs robotics could affect, but based only on capabilities that robots have today.

96
00:10:04,622 --> 00:10:08,612
So not necessarily looking into the future, but examining what is possible today.

97
00:10:08,942 --> 00:10:12,384
The article is titled, What work can robots do?

98
00:10:12,544 --> 00:10:27,844
The research found that robotics can do tasks accounting for 34% of work hours, this is at least in the case of the US, but it's only cost competitive 0.3% of the time.

99
00:10:28,334 --> 00:10:42,116
So they're looking at this exposure of jobs to robotics, but they're not predicting job losses now because it economically doesn't make sense to replace humans, the human workers, in almost all of the cases.

100
00:10:42,958 --> 00:10:52,420
So they defined, just to give you a little bit more context as well, they defined an exposure rubric, which I've pictured here on the top left of the screen.

101
00:10:52,654 --> 00:11:02,464
It goes from E0, which is where the robots can't do a task, and then it goes up where they can do the work even in an unstructured setting.

102
00:11:02,574 --> 00:11:04,260
So something like a city street.

103
00:11:04,622 --> 00:11:14,564
And then there's these mid-levels, E1 and E2, which cover purpose-built spaces for robotics, like within a factory, and also structured workplaces.

104
00:11:15,214 --> 00:11:26,500
Now the bar chart below that rubric shows that 54% of US work time is cognitive or interpersonal, which means that robotics does not touch this.

105
00:11:27,022 --> 00:11:38,980
For the rest of the physical work, though, it says robots can do tasks accounting for 34%, which is what I mentioned before, but this is mostly in these purpose-built or structured environments.

106
00:11:39,342 --> 00:11:44,708
And just 1% of the time in unstructured settings, like on an open road.

107
00:11:45,134 --> 00:11:51,588
Now, lastly, the large table that you see on the right side, this explains the most exposed occupations.

108
00:11:52,864 --> 00:12:02,944
If you look at all of the list on the left side of the table, most of the exposed occupations are drivers or machine operators of some sort.

109
00:12:03,182 --> 00:12:06,916
So things like operating agricultural equipment or tractors.

110
00:12:07,424 --> 00:12:09,796
Most of them fall into that sort of category.

111
00:12:10,222 --> 00:12:14,464
What I found most interesting about the article is this chart that you're seeing now.

112
00:12:14,702 --> 00:12:16,644
This is a radar chart that they used.

113
00:12:17,006 --> 00:12:29,572
So it compares the occupations with tasks exposed to language models in blue, and then tasks exposed to either language models or robots in red.

114
00:12:29,870 --> 00:12:35,584
And you've got all sorts of occupations pictured here: management, finance, legal, sales, etc.

115
00:12:36,142 --> 00:12:42,276
The LLMs affect the cognitive side of work while the robots tackle the physical work.

116
00:12:42,798 --> 00:12:57,156
So if you look across overall the share of work exposed, it rises from around half with language models alone, so the cognitive side, up to 81% when robots are included too.

117
00:12:57,806 --> 00:13:00,304
So this go check out the whole research paper.

