00:19:07,214 --> 00:19:10,020
And this was an announcement that came from Google.

185
00:19:10,510 --> 00:19:15,872
So they have created a population dynamics foundation model.

186
00:19:16,494 --> 00:19:16,992
What is that?

187
00:19:17,166 --> 00:19:19,632
Well, they're combining all these different sorts of data.

188
00:19:19,712 --> 00:19:27,364
They're taking local search trends, they're taking movement patterns, as well as environment and weather data.

189
00:19:27,630 --> 00:19:36,324
They're combining it all together into this foundation model that they call a fingerprint for each place, like for each location.

190
00:19:36,814 --> 00:19:45,604
So researchers can then add these location fingerprints into existing health models to help them create better estimates.

191
00:19:46,158 --> 00:19:58,500
So even when the official health data in some places incomplete or it's delayed, this new foundation model can fill in the missing pieces and make for much more reliable forecasting models.

192
00:19:58,926 --> 00:20:03,152
I'll show you a couple of examples because I think it makes more sense when you see how it's being applied.

193
00:20:03,312 --> 00:20:13,380
So a couple of things that they highlighted at the US-Canada border, they're using it to add more context to estimate better the childhood vaccination rates.

194
00:20:13,966 --> 00:20:24,912
Across the US as well, it's being used to help estimate current heart disease deaths more accurately, especially when the data is delayed for the real figures.

195
00:20:25,166 --> 00:20:28,484
They can use this to have an estimate much earlier.

196
00:20:29,038 --> 00:20:36,196
Also in Mexico, they're using it to improve the forecasts of um dinghy uh hot spots for the disease.

197
00:20:36,686 --> 00:20:45,860
In the US, they're also using it for postpartum depression to add local context into the models to better estimate the risks for new mothers.

198
00:20:46,286 --> 00:20:56,112
And then, as well, in the Democratic Republic of the Congo, they're using it to help them better flag the next likely cholera outbreak area.

199
00:20:56,398 --> 00:21:03,012
So all of this is adding like a new level, a new type of data to health models, something that they never had before.

200
00:21:03,598 --> 00:21:09,552
So up until now, we've seen the angle of how AI can help advance scientific research.

201
00:21:09,806 --> 00:21:15,872
Now I want to look at this from another angle, and we're going to look at the science behind AI.

202
00:21:16,686 --> 00:21:25,120
So the first thing, there's this new research looking at a change to how language models process information.

203
00:21:25,422 --> 00:21:30,640
So most language models today, they read text in pieces called tokens, right?

204
00:21:30,862 --> 00:21:34,400
And a token can be a whole word or it can be parts of a word.

205
00:21:34,480 --> 00:21:38,468
Like unhappy could be two tokens, un plus happy.

206
00:21:38,958 --> 00:21:47,716
So the new idea of what is called bytefication changes how these models process smaller units that make up text.

207
00:21:48,110 --> 00:21:52,900
So looking at the individual bytes of information instead of considering the tokens.

208
00:21:53,358 --> 00:21:57,280
And this could help those models notice when the details matter.

209
00:21:57,422 --> 00:22:08,292
So things when the exact characters in code might matter, or you can imagine, like in biological sequences, when the exact letter being used matters a whole lot.

210
00:22:08,846 --> 00:22:13,892
So allegedly, the existing language models they can be adapted in this way.

211
00:22:14,254 --> 00:22:23,748
And so they can become better at this character level, tasks, but it does not mean that it would make a model better at everything necessarily.

212
00:22:24,078 --> 00:22:28,676
And there's also this risk that it could possibly increase the processing costs.

213
00:22:29,294 --> 00:22:32,100
So now GPT can count the R's in strawberry.

214
00:22:32,320 --> 00:22:36,516
It might help them to be able to count better the letters within the words.

215
00:22:37,230 --> 00:22:41,120
So that's one interesting thing of the science behind AI.

216
00:22:41,280 --> 00:22:47,204
Another thing, and this is moving back into the world model territory.

217
00:22:47,726 --> 00:22:57,760
Researchers in China created a benchmark that tests whether AI video models show measurable physical relationships correctly.

218
00:22:57,934 --> 00:23:02,660
So, like how objects move or how the light bends, connecting back to physics and things.

219
00:23:03,150 --> 00:23:10,404
What they did is they tested eight of the top models across 40 scenarios and they found repeated errors.

220
00:23:10,640 --> 00:23:16,132
Despite the videos looking very convincing, they were still very wrong.

221
00:23:16,878 --> 00:23:19,360
So you can see here, this is pictured from the paper.

222
00:23:19,520 --> 00:23:21,924
The test covered nine areas of physics.

223
00:23:22,160 --> 00:23:32,004
Lots of things like collisions and pendulums, phase transitions like melting, magnets, flowing water, all of these things.

224
00:23:32,480 --> 00:23:48,676
Each scenario checks whether an AI-generated video not just that it looks realistic, but actually that it's complying with physical laws, that the relationships and what is happening in the video upholds to the real world rules.

225
00:23:49,582 --> 00:23:51,280
I'll show you here as well on the screen.

226
00:23:51,470 --> 00:23:54,436
This is showing you the overall performance of the models.

227
00:23:54,702 --> 00:24:04,036
So each bar is a different model and it's how much how much they scored on a specific physics area across those nine that were just pictured before.

228
00:24:04,494 --> 00:24:06,800
Most of the models you'll notice are Chinese.

229
00:24:06,880 --> 00:24:10,720
I've listed who the model owners are across the top of the image.

230
00:24:10,990 --> 00:24:13,840
The only non-Chinese model is from NVIDIA.

231
00:24:14,318 --> 00:24:26,020
Now, if we combine across all of the nine physics scenarios, the best overall score was about 58 out of 100 achieved by one of Byte Dance's models.

232
00:24:26,542 --> 00:24:28,000
Now, this is not a percentage.

233
00:24:28,160 --> 00:24:31,748
This doesn't mean like 58% correct out of 100.

234
00:24:32,110 --> 00:24:36,640
It's just that the way that they created this benchmark scoring, it's designed to be out of 100.

235
00:24:36,720 --> 00:24:39,840
But the score itself, to give you context, is quite low.

236
00:24:40,014 --> 00:24:46,564
What they're saying is that these models are still struggling to get physics consistently right in tests.

237
00:24:46,800 --> 00:24:51,044
It might look okay on a video, but the actual physics happening is not correct.

238
00:24:51,470 --> 00:24:58,596
So I think that the biggest takeaway is that just because a video looks real, it does not guarantee reliable physics.

239
00:24:58,960 --> 00:25:14,884
I think this could be considered good news in one way, because if you think about misinformation and fake news from AI-generated videos, they can still be detected because these videos do not comply with physics rules when you really start to study the details.

240
00:25:15,374 --> 00:25:29,188
But I assume that these problems are going to be solved eventually because you've got all of this work being done on video world models that are either they're going to be able to, at some point, I think, simulate real world physics.

